Triumph of right-wing populists in Austria, they have 29 percent in total
According to the preliminary results, the early parliamentary elections in Austria brought a significant weakening of the two parties forming the current grand coalition, as well as a notable strengthening of populist parties.
The Austrian People's Party (ÖVP), where the position of current President Wilhelm Molterer is seriously threatened, suffered particularly significant losses.
On the other hand, the electoral leader of the Union for the Future of Austria (BZÖ), the governor of Carinthie Jörg Haider, is preparing a triumphant return to the political scene at the national level.
According to the preliminary results, the Socialists (SPÖ) remain, with 29.8 percent of the vote, the country's first political force, however, compared with the 2006 elections, they lost 5.5 percent of the vote.
The ÖVP, which with 25.6 percent loses nearly nine percent of the vote, ended even worse.
This is the worst results of the two major post-war parties; especially for the People's Party, which triggered the early elections, the result is literally catastrophic.
Still in early July, when the ÖVP left the coalition, the People's Party had a considerable lead over the SPÖ.
However, voters visibly chastised them for allowing the government to collapse.
From the first reactions to the results, speculation regarding the possible resignation of the leader of the party and the current vice-chancellor Wilhelm Molterer, was pronounced.
Observers believe that this could happen as early as Monday, during the special council of the party leadership.
Such an evolution would a priori facilitate the path towards the end of the outcome of the election which remains the most likely, namely the renewal of the grand coalition SPÖ and ÖVP.
Nevertheless, given the strengthening of the two right-wing populist parties - the free (FPÖ) reportedly obtained 18 percent and BZÖ eleven percent of the votes - at the same time the Socialists expressed the fear of a rehearsal of 1999, when the people's party agreed with the populists (at the time, the FPÖ was still unified, it was in 2005 that the BZÖ separated from it) on a common government.
Haider, who has already stated that he is ready to collaborate with any party and that he foresees the return to Vienna, on the national political scene, is apparently betting on this evolution.
The last party to have access to the parliament being the Greens, but they have slightly lossed and fell, among the Austrian political parties, from the third in fifth position.
Obviously, no other party crosses the four percent limit and goes to parliament.
Schwarzenberg: Relations with India are likely to cool down
If we couldn't solve the problem concerning the Czech researchers accused, in India, accused of illegal cockroaches, he would ensue, according with Czech Foreign Minister Karel Schwarzenberg, a certain cooling out of Czech-Indian relations.
The minister declared it today in New York, after meeting his Indian counterpart Pranab Mukherjee.
"If the problem wasn't solved, then yes, some cooling would occur," he said after the meeting, following a Czech initiative.
Mukherjee said, on leaving the talks, that the executive power could hardly intervene in court cases.
He nevertheless mentioned that in the case of Petr Švácha, who was discharged from the charge and punished with a fine, the central government could negotiate with the local government, that of Darjeling, the case being placed under the jurisdiction of it.
"His problem could be solved," the minister said.
According to Mukherjee, the best solution for Emil Kučera, sentenced to three years in prison, is to appeal to a higher court.
As a result, he could apply for bail and wait for trial, he said.
Schwarzenberg confirmed that his partner expressed himself in the same direction also during the talks.
"He advises appealing to a higher court, after which he could be pardoned," he said.
The only way, by which the Czech side can intervene in this case, is to provide convicts with "competent legal support", the Indian minister said.
He also said that such help had already been given to them by India as well.
Above all, we want to keep with India the best relations, which have a tradition continuing over many years.
The Indians also acknowledge it,” Schwarzenberg said.
At the same time, as he told ČTK, he pointed out before his partner that the Czech Republic attached great importance to the problem of Czech entomologies.
"We consider the incarceration and accusation of these Czech scholars to be a great despise," he said.
The minister dodged the question if he had obtained from India the promise to deal with the matter.
“I’ve already received quite a large number of promises,” he said only.
A Czech arbitrator the fight for the seafloor
It is approached by an electronic wall map, on which Australia and the adjacent marine areas are represented.
However, he is not interested in the continent, but at a small point lost in the blue of the ocean.
"The island belongs to Australia, as well as the adjacent seabed.
This area, however, is as large as the continent itself," says Italian lawyer Luigi Santosuosso, who is part of the team of thirty members of the UN Division for the Affairs of the Oceans and the Law of the Sea.
Its leader, the director of the division, Czech jurist Václav Mikulka, follows the presentation and completes it from time to time.
Ardour at work and enthusiasm characterize the prevailing atmosphere.
The seabed hides a little-known world so far, rich in natural resources.
Oil, minerals and other mineral resources can be found, but also unknown living organisms for the moment, which may contain the key to the development of a cancer drug.
Substantives larger than land
The coastal states have begun today to vehemently aspire to the new underwater territories.
According to a UN convention, they are indeed using their rights, over which they agreed in 1982 at Montego Bay in Jamaica.
It is mainly the area of so-called continental shelf, which often stretches several hundred kilometers from the coast of the given country.
According to the convention, the latter can claim exclusively to all the resources of the continental shelf.
"To simplify, part of the seafloor and its subsoil, which constitutes a natural extension of the mainland under the sea, is considered a continental shelf.
It extends beyond the limits of the coastal sea, often also beyond the two-hundred-miles marine that represent the so-called exclusive economic yone, "says Mikulka.
The request of the country in question to the Commission for the establishment of the external borders of continental shelf should be supported by extensive scientific-technical documentation based on many costly prospecting and scrutiny.
It is the result of several years of preparatory work.
"One of our roles is to provide legal and scientific-technical aid.
So far, the committee has dealt with five requests from Russia, Brazil, Australia, Ireland and New Zealand.
For the moment, only the last three countries mentioned have been successful,” Mikulka added.
The success of Australia, which is the first to have significantly expanded its underwater possessions, has recently experienced a global impact.
New Zealand, too, has gained territory - the extent of the seaflood allocated to it by the commission last week corresponds to 1.6 million square kilometers, which represents the extent of the country multiplied sixfold.
Both countries have invested millions of dollars in prospecting.
The profit they will derive in the future from the exploitation of the seabed will, as they project themselves, invaluable.
Gradually, other countries will, in turn, apply.
They can do so within ten years after ratifying the "Montego Bay Convention".
There, where the continental shelf ends, begins the area of the ocean floor, proclaimed by convention "common heritage of humanity".
There, no more states have an exclusive position.
The right of extraction in this area shall be assigned by an international institution established by convention.
"However, let's make no mistake about it, the seabed is not a Klondike," said exaggerating Director Mikulka, adding that "its areas are precisely clearly defined and their regime anchored in a contract".
The Czechs in the Pacific
Part of the seabed also belongs to the Czech Republic today.
It shares it with other states of the former bloc of the countries of the East within the framework of the common organization "Interoceanmetal".
It obtained in 1991, under the agreement, as well as investors from a few other states, the status of the so-called "a pioneer investor".
It has the right to extract in the ClarionClipperton zone in the Pacific Ocean, located between Mexico, the Hawai Islands and Kiribati.
The area prospected by the investor slightly exceeds the area of the former Czechoslovakia.
The bottom is eventful, it is a kind of underwater replica of the Tatra.
"It is possible to extract over an area with a total area of 75 000 square kilometers.
The area represents a deposit rich in polymetallic concretions containing nickel, copper, cobalt, and manganese," enumerates Ind Pakshi Rajan, another lawyer.
Rober Sandev, a Macedonian employee of the division, brings an example of such a concretion.
It recalls a small potato, or, with a little imagination, the mythical shoem, which, according to legend, animated the Golem.
The German special forces were there for the release of the hostages
The Egyptian government had sought Germany's help in resolving the hostage crisis - the experts came.
When the situation became serious, the special units were ready, but they have never taken action.
More and more details on the circumstances of the release of eleven European tourists kidnapped in Egypt are revealed.
According to information from the Ministry of the Interior and the Ministry of Defence, the German special forces also participated indirectly in the rescue operation.
For example, both Federal Army specialists and GSG 9 officials from the Federal Police Air Squadron and the Federal Criminal Police Office, as well as logistics experts from the Technischen Hilfwerk were at the place of release.
As the spokesman of the Ministries communicated Monday evening, the German forces were finally not required, the hostage-takers having freed their prisoners and having fled when they acknowledged the imminence of a release by force.
The Egyptian government has reportedly accepted the aid offered by Germany to include it as a support force.
“It didn’t happen so far,” said a spokesman for the Federal Ministry of the Interior.
The aggressors had abducted in southern Egypt the group of tourists composed of five Germans, five Italians, one Romanian and their eight Egyptian escorts.
Following their abduction in the desert in southern Egypt, the hostages were transbaunted in the border area between Egypt, Libya, Sudan and Chad.
The identity of the hostage-takers has so far remained vague, as well as the details of the release or possible ransom payments.
Those who have been rescued have in the meantime arrived safely in Cairo.
Steinmeier said the released hostages were under the protection of the German embassy.
He didn't say anything about the details of the release.
In Berlin at the moment, no information has been given on the date of their return to Germany.
According to "Bild" magazine, the five holidaymakers and special forces should have taken off aboard an Air Force Airbus and arrive in Germany on Tuesday.
United States House of Representatives Denies Bailout
The U.S. Congressional First Chamber narrowly failed the US$700-billion-dollar bailout of heavy financial markets.
The Dow Jones fell into the abyss.
With 227 votes to 2006, the U.S. House of Representatives rejected the bailout in favor of the government’s financial markets.
Despite all the calls of U.S. President George W. Bush and politicians at the head of both parties, the program that was due to curb the chaos of the financial markets did not win the majority.
In a dramatic maneuver, the supporters had desperately tried to convince opponents of the well-founded of this 700 billion-dollar heavy plan.
Barney Frank, one of the Democratic representatives during the debates, had called on MPs for a favorable vote although the plan was unpopular.
“It’s hard to have political recognition for avoiding something that hasn’t happened yet,” he conceded.
During the vote again, the Dow Jones fell by more than six per cent by 600 points.
The reason for this was the concern of investors that the state bailout could collapse.
He went back shortly before the end of the vote, and then, after the announcement of the failure, fell again.
Experts are now projecting further severe turbulence in international financial markets.
In order for the program to come into force, approval and the House of Deputies and the Senate are required.
The organizers report an increasing number of bookings
This should be a good winter for German travel agencies:
In terms of bookings for the coming months according to a study, turnover increased sharply compared to the previous year - for the winter season, even by more than 30 percent.
Despite weak cyclical prospects, Germany’s thirst for travel remains insatiable according to this study.
Regarding reservations for the winter season, the travel agencies recorded a turnover of more than 31 percent compared to the same period the previous year, as the German Federation of Travel Agencies (DRV) told Berlin on Monday.
According to the survey of the GfK Market Research Institute, there are already increasing booking figures dominating in the fall months.
Thus the sales figure would have climbed to about 11 percent in comparison with 2007, confided the DRV.
The sources of these results are GfK figures, which analyzes monthly booking data from 1200 travel agencies and makes estimates for the overall market.
Travel bookings made since the end of August 2008 would be recorded there.
Due to the low price of the dollar, trips to the United States would be particularly in demand, said the DRV.
There, the increase in bookings would amount to about one Third.
Besides this, Spain, Turkey and Greece as well as our country would be destinations particularly sought after by the Germans.
Especially organized trips are in high demand, explained the president of the DRV Klaus Laepple.
In view of the rising prices of energy and food products, the costs here would be "more easily calculable for the customer than for an individually organized trip".
Consumers can save money, among other things, with early booking discounts or All Inclusivie offers, Laepple said.
The summer months too have been highly retained so that an increase in sales figures will again be recorded compared to last year - although according to another study, the number of Germans who undertook a trip fell in comparison with the previous year.
My First Time at the Beer Festival
Ballermann or Apres-Ski's hits continuously, beer in liter pitchers and celebrities in Tyrolean designer costumes.
An event at the beer festival.
Suzanne Kronzucker and I actually don't have much in common.
She's a blonde, a TV host and wears a tailor-made Tyrolian suit.
I've got dark hair. I'm a student and my dress is second-hand.
But one thing connects us: just three minutes at the beer party, and already the photographers throw themselves at us and fill their memory card - very flattering, and this on my first visit to the beer party.
That's how we make friends.
The cheap Tyrolean costume seems to reach its goal.
As a beer festival tourist and normally living north of the Weißwurst equator, my sense of well-being in the traditional costume was until now limited.
But once in Place Thérèse, everything is half less worse.
Almost everyone wears a Tyrolian suit or leather trousers; no one looks jaded or affected by shame - it's completely normal.
Only the apron of my Tyrolean costume gets a few strange shots of eyes at the banana-chocó stand.
A Bavarian woman points out to me that I would have misattached my apron: "to the left means free, to the virgin environment, to the square right, to the widowed back."
Discreetly, I'm tickling my loop from the back to the left.
“It’s better,” she said and laughed.
Others don't give themselves as much trouble for these subtle flirting techniques and wear simple markers, on which is written "Too to get dressed up!" and exposes us their bare hindquarters without being invited.
The young man with the exhibitionist attitude is called Bob, arrives from England and comes for the premiere at the beer festival.
He thinks it's "gorgeous", with an "ooooo" elongated.
He's telling me all this after getting relieved of the mug he'd drank before.
Directly beside this fire hydrant that everyone thinks is a trash can.
Bob is therefore one of these infantly famous "wild pistors".
Others don’t have the chance to get rid of alcohol this way.
Around eight o'clock in the evening, ribambelles of first responders run on the place of the party and must take care of the poor drinkers.
Nurses intervene rather in packs - always by four, they drag their stretchers covered with tents in tents.
It works this way for most visitors of the beer festival: we get there empty, we come back full.
Those who rest in the coffin of the stuffed under the tarpaulin are not visible.
"It's the ones who can't swallow anything anymore," one of the rescuers shouted in response to my question and then disappeared into the fray.
Victim of accidents identified by the police
A 63-year-old pedestrian, who died in the hospital after being hit by a vehicle, has been identified.
Carole Paulson, from Chelmsford Road in Brentwood, was implicated in Friday afternoon's accident in town on Shenfield Road.
She was transported to Queen's Hospital, Romford, but later died there.
Following the accident, a 51-year-old man was arrested on suspicion of dangerous driving.
He's been released on bail ever since.
Turkey is expanding its diplomatic horizons.
Long before Turkey thought of joining the European Union, the European powers were eager to enter its hinterland.
On the east side of the Bosphorus, perhaps 20 steps from Asia is one of the finest relics of its fallen imperial ambition.
Hydarpasha station was designed in a neo-renaissance style by two German architects, Otto Ritter and Helmut Conu.
At least that’s what the guides tell us.
The monument is arranged on horseback at the bottom of the platforms, equipped with observation towers at each of its corners, such as some German provincial town halls.
The stones are dirty.
The whole building's in ruins.
But once inside the vaulted ticketing, we have no more doubt about the size of the company, which represents a passage to the west.
The station was inaugurated in August 1908 by a German association, the Anatolia-Baghdad Corporation.
Germany, retardant to the game of imperialism, wanted to realize its medieval ambitions of domination thanks to this railway that arrives in Damascus, Baghdad as well as the land better known as Saudi Arabia.
But German imperialist ambitions collapsed during World War II.
His railways were attacked by Lawrence of Arabia and his Arabic irregulars.
The relative isolation of its surrounding area, engendered by the icy borders of the Cold War, is over.
. The British and the forces of the empire advanced beyond Egypt and pushed the Turks and their German allies out of Palestine and Syria.
But there was also bad news for Hyderpasha station.
In 1917, a train loaded with ammunition exploded destroying its subtle roof.
Turkish sources say this is sabotage.
Since then, she slowly falls into ruin, engulfed in the meanders of history.
But today, the rail crossing to Anatolia and all the eastern points, at least symbolically, to underline the enlargement of Turkey's diplomatic horizons.
The ruling Justice and Development party, the AKP, has created a new foreign policy for the country.
The relative isolation of its surrounding area, generated by the icy borders of the Cold War
Now it's time for a new policy of commitments.
The Turkish government has remarkable relations with Israel and Syria, the Palestinian factions of Hamas and Fatah, Iran and Iraq, and of course with the European Union and the United States.
To judge the parameters of this new foreign policy, I embarked on a bed car in the direction of the Turkish capital, Ankara, to visit MP AKP Turkish Suat Kiniklioglu, one of the foreign policy experts.
The next morning, after a 10-and-a-half-hour journey, shaken on noisy rails whose cadence reminded the old world of the famous documentary The Night Mail, I finally arrived, in the Turkish capital – presented as a new city of a new centuries-old state, turning its back to its German past.
But the AKP, with its well-known Islamist roots, represents an example of a change in the Turkish consensus.
He suspected the West, and especially in Washington.
A taxi accompanied me to the headquarters of the AKP, an ultra-modern structure, expressly built of stones and steel, in a developing neighborhood, surrounded by half-built shopping centers and by many pastries in which middle managers in costume-tie-men and women - meet for morning affairs.
Suat Kiniklioglu could have been a centre-right politician from any other party in Europe.
Costume, tie and he even has a Black Berry.
"the purpose of this foreign policy is to reintegrate Turkey now into its immediate vicinity.
“The Cold War was an anomaly,” he said.
Turkey has been cut off from its hinterland.
Now she has to look east and west at the same time.
This was not the case for both of them.
She had to do both.
"But, now, at some point, I asked if" Turkey shouldn't she choose?"
"Turkey," he said, "don't want to have to choose between the two."
He then pointed out an aspect that almost all those with whom I have been able to speak have recited to me by heart: The Turkish railway goes not only to the Middle East, but also to the Caucasus, and around the Black Sea, it is therefore, as much an advantage for Europe and the United States as for Turkey itself.
With the integration of Turkey, the message seems to draw a Europe traveling metaphorically on the tracks to Asia and the Middle East.
Returning to Hydarpasha Station, after another night without sleeping, I rethought these two Germans, Herr Ritter and Herr Conu, who followed the construction of this building 100 years ago.
They would have been surprised to see that the European ambitions of the Middle East could, in large part, be in Turkey’s hands.
As I waited at the terminal, the ferry across the Bosphorus, I remembered the period during which Turkey had been named "the patient of Europe".
At least there is no longer any doubt on the continent that could claim this fascinating country.
Memoirs of Vernon Handley and William Woodruff.
Mark Brown writes: Music director at Guildford since 1962, Vermon "Tod" Handley (died 9/11) was in charge of the Guildford Philharmonic Orchestra and Choir.
Already part of his musical project for the city, he founded the following year, the fantastic choir of young people, Proteus.
The choir had to include those under 21s, but before Proteus could sing his last song in 1979, many of us who were more than 20 years old—refused to abandon the choir.
The denomination "Proteus" (which changed shape according to mythology) represented the choir's willingness and ability to manage any type of music was presented to him, whether madrigal or Messianic.
The musical versatility produced by such a young choir was often impressive, always exciting, thanks to the inspiration of one man, and his enthusiasm for music, which remained constant over the years.
Remind Yourself to how much Tod told us to "leave that the music speaks for itself".
He reportedly said, "The composer knows what he wants"
It's all there in music.
So sing!"
His baguette technique was absolutely clear, precise, even if ever dictatorial or appearing.
Regardless of the musical standard, he raised it much higher.
John Frayn Turner writes:
If a driver were to be named a knight, it's Vernon Handley.
I knew him well during the decades from 1962 to 1982, when he drove the Guildford Philharmonic.
For the past 40 years, I've seen more than a hundred of his concerts, and I can honestly say that none of them has ever disappointed me - and, a few times, they've even inspired me
Tod Handley held this combination of greatness and personal modesty so rare.
He hated drivers who, according to him, "only focus on certain people."
He'll miss many of us.
William Woodruff
Professor John Beckett wrote:
Your obituary of William Woodruff (died September 25) states that "in 1946, he had resumed his studies in economic history, in Oxford, and in 1950 became a member of Houblon-Norma research, while he was doing a BSc, an MA and a PhD".
That's what's a letter from Woodruff's author in Beyond Nab End, but it's not correct.
From 1946 to 1953 he was a professor of economic history at the University of Nottingham, and at the same time completed a PhD thesis on the British plastic industry, under the supervision of Professor J.D. Chambers; it was later published in 1958 as The British Rubber Industry.
In the 1950s and 51 in the United States, he was studying at the Norman Hop, and in the summer of 1952, at the Universities of Illinois, Harvard and New York.
In 1953 he left Nottingham for Illinois.
The reason Woodruff chose to hide that part of his past remains unknown.
J. Vicente Resino at the Faculty of Philosophy
"I recommend José Vicente Resino's exhibition "the Flamenco and life" because his black and white photographs, a documentary style, are fantastic.
They caught my attention because of their simplicity and the strength they transmit and because they always treat the protagonists with great respect.
An exhibition that conveys a lot of feelings.
Resino knows how to capture magical moments.”
Second Assault for Honda Super Sales
In November, Honda will begin commercializing the second generation of the Jazz model.
If its predecessor already had good habitability, this new monovolume has an even wider interior space.
To this is added a few refined mechanics, which increase driving comfort and save more gasoline.
An evolution in the face of a revolution.
That’s the second generation of Honda Jazz.
Although aesthetically the new Honda Jazz has a line very similar to the previous model, there are many changes introduced in this monovolume.
Among them, there is an innovative design, a new platform, larger dimensions and new engines.
However, before considering the new model, it should be noted that this new Honda Jazz arrives six years after the release of the first generation.
First of all, it can be seen that Honda has renovated this model in a shorter period than that of the other manufacturers.
Althougher in Europe, the Honda Jazz is less well-known, this model is a sales phenomenon in other markets.
This is the case in Japan, where the Honda Jazz was the most marketed car in 2002 and also in 2008.
Precisely in Japan, nearly a million Honda Jazz has been sold since its launch.
The Old Continent, with more or less a million cars sold in six years is in second place.
However, Honda claims that this new Jazz will allow it to increase sales in Europe.
Thus, while in 2007 the Japanese brand marketed 83,000 Honda Jazz, in 2009 it expects to sell 100,000, which would represent a growth in sales of 17% in the European market.
In the Spanish market, Honda has marketed 8,000 Jazz since 2002, accounting for 6.7% of Honda’s sales in Spain.
Heads of the Spanish subsidiary hope to sell 2,000 Jazz in one year.
It is precisely the release of the new Jazz that will allow Honda to make up largely for the decline in sales in Spain.
Indeed, the managers of the brand hope to end the year with a sale of 22,000 cars, i.e. 4,000 cars less than in 2007, a decrease in sales of 16%.
Thanks to this increase in sales, Honda increased its market share in Spain, reaching a market share of 1.8% compared to 1.6% last year.
The Japanese brand also hopes to obtain a market share of 2% in 2010.
Honda hopes to get this market share when the new model comes out.
It’s Insight, a five-door hybrid with a compact size – 4.37 meters in length – on which the Japanese company hopes to market nearly 200,000 cars per year around the world.
Depending on the markets and according to the Japanese manufacturer's estimates, 50% of Insight's sales will take place in the United States, while the other remaining half will be divided between the Old Container and Japan.
The commercial launch of Insight is planned in the first three months of next year, following its presentation to the public in January in Valencia.
Compared to the first generation, the new Jazz increases its dimensions.
Its length reaches 3.90 meters (five centimeters more), its width 1.69 meters (two centimeters more) and the distance between its axes is 2.50 meters which represents an increase of five centimeters.
On the other hand, the height does not change or 1.52 meters.
Dimensions also increase and the interior space.
On the one hand, this gain is due, on the one hand, to the introduction of a new platform and, on the other hand, to the introduction of a reserve of gasoline in a central position, a solution also adopted for the Honda Jazz.
In the same way, the capacity of the trunk – which has a dual bottom system – can hold up to 399 liters, 19 liters more capacity compared to the first-generation trunk.
It should be noted that these dimensions are those of the version with a 1.4L engine, since the trunk capacity of the Honda Jazz 1.2L is 379 liters.
The other innovation - in addition to external elements such as taillights or panoramic roof - is the improvement of access to the rear seats.
Doors that give access to rear seats have an opening angle of 80 degrees, with respect to the rear doors of the first Jazz that opened with an angle of 67 degrees.
Engines are also new to this second generation of Jazz.
At the beginning of its commercialization, this small model will be equipped with two gasoline thrusters of 1.2 and 1.4 liters.
Both engines feature i-VTEC technology that reduces fuel consumption and CO2 emissions, makes the engine more powerful and increases engine speed.
The first 1.2-litre engine develops a power of 90 horsepower at 6,000 rpm and 114 Nm of torque at 4,900 rpm.
This thruster - which on the previous Jazz reached 78 horsepower - has a fuel consumption of 5.3 liters per 100 km and emissions of 125 grams of CO2.
Compared to the previous thruster, which on the previous generation reached 83 horses, now reaches 100 horses.
This 100-horsepower Honda Jazz 1.4L has a combined consumption of 5.4 liters per 100 km and emissions of 128 grams of CO2.
‘Bee Car’ That Avoids Collisions
To advance the development of technologies for accident prevention, Nissan was inspired, this time, by nature, especially bees.
The Japanese company has created a robot that, by imitating bee theft, avoids accidents.
It’s called Bio-Mimetic Car Robot Drive, or BR23C and represents the first steps towards collision prevention technologies.
Engineers at the Advanced Technical Center of Nissan Motor Co. made this small robot in collaboration with the Center for Advanced Science and Technology Research at the prestigious University of Tokyo, Japan.
According to Kazuhiro Doi, the managing director of Public Relations for Technology and Brand Management Research and Development, the BR23C represents the rise of the Nissan safety shield.
This robot will allow the brand's vehicles to prevent collisions.
During its flight, each bee creates its own personal space of an oval shape that closely resembles the Nissan safety shield.
But the most important thing is the composed eyes of the bee, able to see in an angle of more than 300 degrees and in all directions, which allows it to fly uninterruptedly inside its own personal space.
To recreate this visual function, the engineers have developed the so-called Laser Range Finder.
It detects obstacles over a distance of two meters and within a radius of 180 degrees, calculates the distance to this object and sends a signal to the microprocessor, immediately, to avoid the obstacle.
“As it detects an obstacle, the small robot will mimic the movements of a bee and immediately change direction by turning its wheels 90 degrees or more to avoid the collision.
The biggest difference from any other existing system is that the escape maneuver is totally instinctive.
If this were the case, the robot wouldn’t be able to react quickly enough to avoid obstacles,” explains Toshiyuki Andoh, director of the Displacement Laboratory and principal engineer of this project.
Contrary to a bee, the robot cannot be diverted upwards or downwards, nor diagonally; it can only move in two dimensions and depending on what its wheels allow and not in infinite directions like bees.
For this reason, Nissan used a rotation function, but also acceleration and deceleration, so that the small robot could avoid obstacles.
“Each second this device must only process information and act according to this data.
It doesn’t need to keep the information or the intuitive logics of prior maneuvers, which is why it can operate continuously with only a few dozens KB of memory.
This may be surprising, but we’re not talking about gigabytes of memory, or even megabytes.”
This represents the first-facilition of the development of new technologies for the instantaneous prevention of collisions, a technology that will be introduced into production cars in a decade after Nissan.
The robot works with lithium-polymer batteries, which are lighter and last longer than lithium-ion batteries.
The other possible application of this technology in the future could be the adaptation of this device on a GPS satellite navigation system to guide the driver to his destination by avoiding obstacles on the road.
They're afraid they're mortgaged
It must be acknowledged that the Treasury of the United States, in addition to having a lot of cash to make plans, is not devoid of geniuses.
Faced with the crisis, and to prevent Americans from embarrassinginging mortgages with solvency banks, they have created a game to learn not to fail: Bad Credit Hotel.
That's to say 'the hotel of bad credits' ... or stories.
The gloomy aspect of this accommodation and especially the Maître d'Hôtel leads us to think several times if it is the right time to ask for a loan.
And, above all, where to do it.
Of course, this game gives useful but undetailed information.
It's more of a game for teenagers.
