Morgan Stanley Opens Capital at MUFG
Morgan Stanley has announced the entry of Mitsubishi UFJ Financial Group (MUFG), Japan’s first bank, which will disburse nine billion dollars to take 21% of its capital, a nearly welcomed by a sharp decline in its stock price.
A week after unveiling an interim deal, the two banks explained that MUFG would buy 9.9% of Morgan Stanley common shares at a unit price of US$25.25, a total amount of US$3 billion.
The selected price is 19% less than the book value of the securities as of August 31.
MUFG will invest an additional US$six billion in the acquisition of convertible securities that provide a return of 10 percent with a conversion price of US$31.25.
Morgan Stanley said the overall transaction would be based on an average unit price of US$29, 28% above its current price.
However, Morgan Stanley lost 6.79% to US$23.07 around 17:00 GMT.
“This strategic alliance is an important opportunity to accelerate Morgan Stanley’s transition to banking holding status,” said John Mack, chief executive officer.
Morgan Stanley passed this new statute last week, which should allow it to diversify its activities but bring it under the authority of the Federal Reserve.
His alliance with MUFG also confirms that the current financial crisis is forcing Wall Street’s biggest names to adopt a much more cautious attitude than before.
The modalities presented Monday also show that the agreement has been changed in recent days: last Monday, Morgan had simply announced that he would sell up to 20% of his shares in Japanese for about 8.5 billion dollars.
“A week ago,” Toshihide Mizuno, MUFG’s executive director, told Tokyo, “we thought the stake would be made up entirely of common shares.
However, in view of the current situation of the economy and the market, we wanted to minimise the risks and cover ourselves."
Based on the weighted average price, MUFG will pay its Morgan Stanley 7% securities below their book value.
And the operation will result in a dilution of about 20% of the profit per share.
The agreement provides that MUFG will be able to convert half of its preferential securities into common shares within one year if they exchange more than 50% above the conversion price, at about US$47, for a given period.
Morgan Stanley assures that MUFG's capital will allow it to seize investment opportunities, strengthen its balance sheet and increase liquidity.
A spokeswoman for the group, however, declined to elaborate on the strategic plans of the two new allies.
After MUFG’s investment, Morgan Stanley is expected to display a Tier One solvency ratio of about 15%, one of the best in the industry.
Morgan said it has "clearly" eased its balance sheet since the end of August.
The last few weeks have been challenging for the group, which has seen its stock price come halved while the cost of insurance against a risk of default on its debt soared.
This cost fell on Monday but remained at a high level, around 12%.
Last week, Goldman Sachs, the only “survivor” of the major investment banks of Wall Street after the bankruptcies of Bear Stearns and Lehman Brothers and the acquisition of Merrill Lynch, announced to have raised 15 billion dollars, partly from Berkshire Hathaway, Warren Buffett’s group.
Rafael Correa in Quito on Monday.
Rebuilding the Republic around 21st Century Socialism.
This is the joint project of the Venezuelan Hugo Chávez, the Bolivian Evo Morales and the Ecuadorian Rafael Correa.
This Sunday, like his counterparts and friends, it is the third that put to voice a draft new Constitution.
Mission accomplished since 64% of voters voted "yes" in the referendum, according to official results covering 80% of the votes.
According to these results, the "no" won 28%, 7% of the votes were void and 0.7% were white votes.
In order for the draft Constitution to pass, it had to obtain half of the votes plus one.
On Sunday, two polls carried out at the end of the polling stations and a count of a non-governmental organization had announced a large victory of the "yes", ranking between 63% and 70%.
Rafael Correa had also claimed in the night from Sunday to Monday the victory of his draft Constitution.
"The "Constitution has triumphed overwhelmingly", he assured, congratulating the Ecuadorians at the siege of the governorate of Guayaquil, 280 kilometers southwest of Quito.
As of course, Rafael Correa was congratulated by Chávez and Morales but also his Chilean counterpart Michelle Bachelet.
The election, which took place calmly according to the observation mission of the Organization of American States (OAS), focused on a text in 444 points consolidating the "new socialism" defended by the president.
The draft Constitution enshrines the power of the state over the economy, reserving to it the "planning of the development" of a country living mainly from the extraction of oil by foreign companies and the currencies sent by its emigrants.
Supported by Correa, elected in 2006, the project also grants reinforced powers to the Head of State, who will be able to carry out up to two consecutive four-year terms and will have the opportunity to dissolve the Assembly.
Correa has already announced that he will use this power and seek a new mandate in February 2009.
The Constitution also consolidates the principle of free health and education services that the president has begun to establish in this country of 13.9 million inhabitants, where 50% of the population lives below the poverty line.
Finally, it prohibits the presence of foreign troops, which will put an end to the agreement between Quito and Washington for the use of an Ecuadorian base as a starting point for U.S. anti-drug operations.
The 19 hostages have been released in Chad
An Egyptian commando launched the assault on the kidnappers of the eleven tourists and their eight escorts captured ten days ago.
They're in Cairo.
The eleven European tourists and their eight Egyptian escorts held captive for ten days in the desert were brought back this Monday afternoon safely in the Egyptian capital.
In the morning they had been released by an Egyptian commando in Chad.
Apparentially fit, the hostages arrived around 3:30 p.m. aboard a special plane at the military base of al-Maza, adjacent to Cairo International Airport.
They were immediately transferred, without being able to talk to the press, aboard helicopters to Maadi hospital, a residential suburb of Cairo, for examinations.
They are in "good health", and "no ransom has been paid" to the kidnappers," Egyptian Tourism Minister Zoheir Garanna said on the tarmac.
Captured on September 19 during a 4x4 safari at the foot of Mount Ouanat, on the borders of Egypt, Sudan and Libya, this group of hostages had been tossed from one cache to another by 35 unknown kidnappers.
It includes 11 tourists - five Italians, five Germans and one Romanian - as well as eight Egyptians: two guides, four drivers, a border guard and the director of the Aegyptous agency, organizer of the safari.
An Egyptian security official said that 30 members of the special forces, transported on board two helicopters, launched the assault in the night just before dawn.
"An exchange of gunfire occurred, and half of the captors were killed, with the others fleeing," the official said.
He said that 150 members of the Egyptian special forces of the "lightning" unit had in total been sent to Sudan, where they received support from German and Italian commandos.
Italian Foreign Minister Franco Frattini mentioned from Belgrade, where he is, the role played by secret agents and elements of the Italian special forces.
“We must recognize that we have achieved this result thanks to the professionalism and efficiency of our secret service men, special forces,” he said.
He also denied the payment of a ransom.
The day before, the Sudanese army claimed to have intercepted during a patrol a 4x4 vehicle on which the captors were, killing in six, including the Chadian leader, and arresting two.
The army had claimed that they were linked to a rebellious group in Darfur, the Sudan-Unity Liberation Movement (SLA-U), which this movement had immediately denied.
Letters - The Silent Majority of Culture
A debate on the financing of the cultivation makes headlines and occupies this election campaign.
So much the better.
However, an area of our cultural life is always forgotten.
Who will rise to denounce the sums of misery given to small museums, archives, archaeological excavations or starving national historic sites?
What politician will dare to remind the federal government that it is neglecting its monuments and heritage buildings?
That in Quebec, as in Canada, we do not have real laws effectively preserving our heritage?
Did you know that the French government devotes three and a half million euros to the restoration of Chinon's only castle in the Loire Valley?
Among our American neighbors, it is written in the Constitution of their country that the protection of heritage is part of the culture of Americans and that this heritage must be protected by all citizens, all elected officials and all government departments, in consultation.
In Quebec, at the Fédération des sociétés d'histoire du Québec, at least 40 000 volunteers grouped together in 211 societies of history, heritage, archival and genealogy are concerned with excavating history, preserving with three under heaps of private archives and preserving archaeological remains.
It is they, often, who are the watchdogs of our heritage, who protest with the energy of despair when promoters and decision-makers launch their bulldozers against our old stones and forgotten cemeteries.
To when, a forum for history and heritage?
Benelux governments nationalize part of Fortis bank
The Benelux states assist the Belgian-Dutch bank, as the first European banking institution in distress following the snowball effect of the credit market crisis in the United States, by partially nationalizing it. The three countries bear, up to a sum of 11.2 billion euros, this financial and insurance institution also owning interests in Hungary.
This weekend, the Belgian, Luxembourg and Dutch governments agreed, at the highest level, in this case, by also appealing to the European Central Bank and the head of competition of the European Commission.
In the eurozone, the first major crisis in the context of the international financial crisis for more than a year, is for Fortis. From a certain point of view, the decisions that will be taken in the case of this bank will serve as a precedent.
In accordance with the decision taken in the night of Sunday, the three states will buy back nearly half (up to 49%) of the capital of the bank located in each of the countries.
The bank will undoubtedly sell part of its holdings, among which, in the first place the activities, mainly of insurance, that it acquired last year from the Dutch organization ABN Amro.
Last week, Fortis stocks began a dizzying fall. In the second half of the week, serious signs of crisis have already been revealed in the bank.
Analysts agree that with its 85,000 employees, the bank is far too big for it to be allowed to go bankrupt.
On Friday alone, the company's shares fell by 12 percent, reaching its lowest level 14 years ago.
Since January, stocks have fallen by more than two-thirds of their value.
Taking into account the current value of the shares, the total value of the capital of the group is around 14 billion euros, which, according to the company's managers, is unrealistic.
The management of the company accuses the uncertainty across the Atlantic, in connection with the adoption of the U.S. 700 billion-dollar rescue plan, of being at the origin of the fall of Fortis shares.
On Monday, the papers have already benefited from a slight increase.
On Friday, Filip Dierckx was appointed head of the bank as the new managing director.
The financial organization was also looking for private acquirers, but has not yet received a substantive proposal.
Previously, the Belgian government announced that investors should not worry, they were not going to lose their money.
No panic was felt in the bank's customer circle and no agency was stormed.
In Hungary, Fortis owns independent agencies and has also acquired leasing companies.
A fox in the chicken coop
In America, life and health insurance companies frequently refuse to sign an insurance contract with an applicant, based on information regarding their drug purchases.
Information protection experts are concerned that the data market will become uncontrollable.
"There are data protection programmes which link the use of information concerning the prescription of medicinal products, in any case, to the consent of the data subject.
This kind of program must be in independent hands, otherwise it amounts to entrusting its chicken coop to a fox," Richard Dicket, the creator of the program used to manage drug prescriptions, told the Washington Post.
Insurance companies make the insurance premium and insured capital dependent in the event of damage, the state of health of the client who presents themselves, and if they believe that the risk is too great, they do not even insure it.
In the past, they sent the applicants to a medical examination, and they only signed an insurance contract with them after becoming aware of the results.
For ten years, Dick has found a much cheaper procedure: from an identification number, he is easily provided with pharmacies, the data concerning the purchases of drugs from the person in the eighteen months. From this, the program determines itself, what disease the person suffers from and what is his state of health.
For example, if this person regularly buys high doses of cholesterol reducer, this refers to an extremely high level of fat in the blood, which immediately puts them in a higher risk category.
Several versions of this program are in circulation. At the same time, some determine a risk coefficient, therefore, there is not much left to do to the insurer apart from paperwork. However, tiny problems may still appear.
Among the disadvantages of this program is the fact that a drug can be used for different purposes. For example, some mixtures can be used against migraine or hot flashes, but can also be effective against depression.
The latter is considered by insurers as an aggravating risk factor, while others do not.
Companies have developed in the research and analysis of the data. They have to request permission from the patient before collecting the necessary information from the pharmacy.
From now on, they are constrained by the authorities, even if they have not been punished for their failures in the past.
Recently, some companies are also dealing with the results of laboratory analyses, and linking them with the data on drug use. In this way, they are able to provide an even more accurate picture of the health status of patients.
More and more companies have access to data, and usage control is becoming increasingly difficult.
There is a separate law on the protection of medical data, but its scope extends only to practitioners and insurers.
A proposal for a law on the expansion of the supervisory circle of the authorities concerning companies specializing in the provision of data is currently before Congress.
Dick Richard acknowledges that this is not enough and that is why he has developed even further his program, which would condition the transmission of data to the exclusive agreement of the person concerned.
It is another question of whether the person concerned is really free to give his agreement or not, since if he refuses, insurance does not sign an insurance contract with her.
Thus and in the end, chickens depend on the benevolence of the fox.
The overall menu of Marseille
The European Union and India are halving their trade.
Since the European Union and India are very important trading partners for each other, this theme also dominated the agenda of this ninth summit alongside the other, largely global issues.
Today, a France-India summit will also take place, which will focus on cooperation on atomic energy.
The European Union recognises that India is a major emerging democratic power that should be given a broader right of speech in international affairs, but member countries have not yet succeeded in agreeing on their support in New Delhi for its candidacy as a permanent member of the UN Security Council.
Moreover, they did not dwell too much on this issue, yesterday, at this 9th summit held in Marseille.
The French president, Nicolas Sarkozy, like the current president of the Union, as well as the president of the Commission José Manuel Barroso and the head of diplomacy, Javier Solana, welcomed the Indian Prime Minister, Manmohan Singh, with whom they agreed that in five years, they were going to double trade, which means an increase of a hundred billion euros.
Taking into account that between 2000 and 2007, the exchange of goods has increased from 25 billion to 55 billion, this does not appear to be an overly ambitious goal.
In this sense, they have harmonized from a legal point of view the 26 bilateral agreements with India concerning civilian flights. In this way, air exchanges will also be able to flourish in the future.
India would like to sign a free trade agreement with the Union by the end of 2009.
Sarkozy has vowed to expedite negotiations.
The objective is to balance the situation with, on the one hand, India being the EU's largest trading partner, and on the other, New Delhi, which is only ninth among the EU's partners.
The summit devoted its attention to global affairs.
The Afghan and Pakistani situation has been strongly condemned, as well as all forms of terrorism, and it has been referred to the urgency of holding a United Nations conference on this issue.
The completion of the Doha Trade Round has also been requested, although both have a large share in the current failure.
The EU has confirmed that it remains faithful to the energy and climate programme, in particular that it would like to reduce its releases of toxic materials by at least twenty per cent until 2020.
India also agreed on this point, as well as the fact that a global market would have to pass at next year's climate conference, and reduce greenhouse gas emissions by fifty per cent until 2050.
They would also like to prevent rising food prices, and supported convening an international working meeting to address the financial crisis.
India’s head-of-state travels today to the French capital, where during a bilateral meeting he prepares to sign an agreement on the peaceful use of atomic energy.
In Paris, it is hoped that French companies will receive orders worth twenty billion euros for the construction of Indian atomic reactors.
According to information from the news agency, the agreement would cover 15 years.
In Frankfurt, more than three hundred Italian publishers
Strong presence of Italian publishers at the Frankfurt International Book Fair.
More than three hundred Italian publishers present at the 60th edition of the Buchmesse, the most important international meeting in the exchange of rights.
The Frankfurt International Book Fair awaits for this edition, more than 7 thousand exhibitors from 101 various countries that will occupy the 171,790 square meters of the Salon with more than 403 thousand titles in exhibition, and Turkey as guest of honor.
It will be the Sub-Secretary of the Ministry of Property and Cultural Activities, Francesco Maria Giro, who will represent the Italian Government, at the inauguration ceremony of the Italian Butterfly: indeed, Italy will be present in an institutional way, with a Point Italy- 264 square meters of exhibition space.
It will be followed by the appointment with the press in the Room Struktur, the butterfly 5.0 of the Buchmess: an opportunity to present the figures, trends and foresights on the position of the edition in Italy.
According to the data, the importance of foreign author's books gradually decreases relative to the totality of Italian production, and in particular, compared to that for adults and children.
Steve Fossett's plane, the billionaire missing a year ago.
Steve Fosset's plane wreck, the billionaire missing on September 3, 2007 while flying on the Nevada desert, has been found.
Debris was found on a 3000-metre-high plateau in the Sierra Nevada area of Mammonth Lakes, on the California-Nevada border.
Inside the plane, no human remains however.
Madera County sheriff John Anderson ousted the remaining doubts: "The plane has been located, we've sent a team, and now we have the confirmation that it's the one Steve Fosset was traveling on the day he disappeared," he said at a press conference.
The sheriff specified that no human remains were found either on the plane or in the vicinity.
In February, the 63-year-old billionaire was considered dead.
The U.S. Transportation Safety Board (Ntsb) reported that a few hours after the wreckage, a team of investigators sent to the scene, identified the aircraft: it is indeed the Bellanca 8kcab, which disappeared on September 3, 2007, when Fosset took off from Yerington, Nevada for a three-hour flight.
Yesterday, still in California, not far from the Nevada border, some of Fosset's personal belongings were found by a couple around their Mammoth Lakes home.
These are two documents bearing the name Fosset, both issued by the Federal Aviation Administration of Illinois, cash and a sweater.
Fossett's research was halted one month after he disappeared.
The billionaire had earned a place in history thanks to a long series of records.
On the sixth attempt, he had become in 2002, the first man to complete the round-the-world course on an aerostatic balloon: two weeks of solo flying.
Three years later, he toured the world by plane and non-stop.
Among all these records, also that of having flown at 15,445 meters high, on the Andes, but with a co-pilot.
Glk, the advertisement you don't expect.
An unusual 4x4 of an original and well-defined design, with marked lines, wide surfaces and sharp angles.
Here, in synthesis, Glk, the latest in the "compact" off-road of the house of the star, with which Mercedes-Benz tried to give a wind of novelty in this sector.
Precisely because of its non-standard, unexpected and unconventional content will be the communication that will accompany the launch of the 4x4 Glk, available in the Mercedes-Benz showroom, from October 17.
Thus the hype that will accompany the beginning of the new car will give rise to an unprecedented modality of communication, through a futuristic campaign of 3D displays, used for the first time in Italy by an automotive brand.
A max displays project, articulated in three different phases, which will dominate the heart of Milanese shopping, Corso Como, until November 15.
The first phase of this new type of communication foresees the presentation of the car in stereoscopic vision, and thanks to special glasses distributed around Corso Como, it will be possible to see the new 4x4 in almost real dimension and in three-dimensional format.
Indeed, we will have the impression that by looking at the image, the auto comes out of the poster.
Then, through the use of Elimina8 innovative technology, the new Glk will be illuminated according to the spots, in all its details, and then recomposed into a single image.
It is obvious that with this advertisement, Mercedes is trying to introduce something new into the stereotypical field of motorist communication.
Will she succeed with this formula in arousing the interest of the future acquirers? We will see.
These are Italian hooligans.
Triveneto, they're risking Daspo.
But who are these 150-200 young boys and girls of which Italy is ashamed now? They are part of the group "Ultras Italy" which follows the Italian national team for years, and which, until now, had never created big problems.
Nor had it ever clashed with the opposing tifosi.
The extraction of the group is on the right.
Promoters come from Triveneto.
From Verona, Padua, Trieste, and Udine.
In Sofia, there have been small groups (really) of Campania and Puglia.
They had tricolor flags on which their city of origin appeared: Saturday night, they were all abducted so as not to be identified.
They are inspired by English tifosery.
In Bulgaria, they found a tiffosery of real Nazis, that of the Levsky.
La Figc had made available Italian tifosi, 144 tickets for the part: all nominatives, and therefore easily controllable.
And the Viminal had indeed controlled them all: among those who left for Sofia, none had a Daspo in course, none had been forbidden to attend a stadium.
Four Italian tifosi who had a history of stadium crime, did not have the right to the ticket, and could not make the trip.
Now the Viminal is waiting to read the acts that will happen to the Bulgarian police, and will then take measures: in this case, it may be that tifosi arrested or identified in the disorders will purge a Daspo for at least five years.
An international Daspo that forbids frequenting Italian stadiums, but also European.
In short, our hooligans will no longer set foot in the stadium.
Luckily.
But now it's time for fear-emulation: that is, others are likely to add to the first one hundred and fifty.
"We must extinguish this fire immediately, and not legitimize who behaves in this way," argues the Viminal.
So, "keep your eyes open especially in view of Wednesday's national team game in Lecce"
In Apulia is indeed a small nucleus of supporters of the Ultras Italy.
Tomorrown, meets the Observatoire, Tuesday the Casms which can even count on a section of the Aise, i.e. of the secret services, which deal precisely with stadium problems.
"Duce-Duce choirs, and the outstretched arm during Mameli's anthem? In Bulgaria, it's not a crime"
Here is the opinion (expressed in a really unpleasant way) of Domenico Mazzilli, recently, director of the Viminal Observatory (he took the position of Francesco Tagliente and then Felice Ferlizzi).
Mazzilli was formerly prefect in Trieste, where there is a nucleus of supporters of the Ultras Italy group, founded in 2002.
It is true, as Mazzilli says, that in Bulgaria, renting Mussolini is not a crime, but it is certain that the behavior of these 150-200 tifosis has been shameful.
"I am not a sociologist - adds the No. 1 of the Observatory - the crimes must be attributed in the country in which the fact occurs.
Until now, this group had stood by, now we'll see what happened, and we'll evaluate for the future.
But let's also talk about the whistles towards Mameli's hymn: these too are not crimes, but if you allow me, they are not polite..."
